WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.803
Two inputs arrive 10 ms apart.

2
00:00:06.703 --> 00:00:10.023
Does the second start from rest, or from what the first left behind?

3
00:00:11.117 --> 00:00:15.277
When conductances change, the marker does not jump to the new balance point.

4
00:00:16.097 --> 00:00:17.237
It moves there gradually.

5
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:23.090
Changing the voltage means moving charge onto or off the membrane's capacitance.

6
00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:24.910
That takes time.

7
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:28.710
The membrane time constant, τ, says how long.

8
00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:33.030
It is the time to get 63% % of the way to a new voltage.

9
00:00:34.083 --> 00:00:38.503
τ equals the capacitance divided by the total open conductance.

10
00:00:39.163 --> 00:00:41.043
In our cell, that is 20 ms.

11
00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:47.787
Because τ uses the total conductance, in this passive model every synapse that opens

12
00:00:47.787 --> 00:00:48.787
shortens it.

13
00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.210
So an EPSP has a shape.

14
00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:57.170
For a brief, fast input in our cell, it rises while synaptic current flows, over a

15
00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:58.170
few milliseconds.

16
00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:03.790
Once that current fades, the voltage decays back toward rest, with the resting time

17
00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:04.790
constant.

18
00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:10.390
If a second EPSP arrives before the first has gone, it starts from the first one's

19
00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.750
tail. That is temporal summation.

20
00:01:13.783 --> 00:01:17.483
Two brief, idealized pulses arrive 10 ms apart.

21
00:01:18.383 --> 00:01:20.563
Each jumps the voltage by 2 mV.

22
00:01:21.443 --> 00:01:23.163
Cell A has a τ of 20 ms.

23
00:01:24.363 --> 00:01:26.843
Cell B's is 10 Which ends higher?

24
00:01:32.917 --> 00:01:39.637
Cell A. After 10 ms, its first EPSP has decayed only to about 61%.

25
00:01:39.637 --> 00:01:42.637
percent. The second lands on top of that.

26
00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:47.930
Cell B's first EPSP has fallen to about 37%.

27
00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:52.710
So cell A ends a little over 3 mV, and cell B under 3

28
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.440
A longer τ leaves a longer tail, and that means more summation.

29
00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.800
Remember cell B. It returns later in this lesson.

30
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.660
Now distance. Most synapses sit on dendrites, not on the cell body.

31
00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:11.743
Current entering a dendrite flows toward the soma.

32
00:02:12.183 --> 00:02:14.603
But some leaks out across the membrane along the way.

33
00:02:15.583 --> 00:02:20.823
In a long, uniform passive dendrite, a steady voltage change shrinks by the same

34
00:02:20.823 --> 00:02:22.603
fraction over each equal stretch.

35
00:02:23.617 --> 00:02:28.137
The length constant, λ, is the distance over which it falls to 37%.

36
00:02:28.137 --> 00:02:29.137
percent.

37
00:02:29.617 --> 00:02:32.457
Put a synapse half a length constant from the soma.

38
00:02:33.077 --> 00:02:36.317
About 61% percent of its steady depolarization arrives.

39
00:02:37.417 --> 00:02:42.657
A brief EPSP loses more, because capacitance along the way also filters it.

40
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.900
Adding inputs at different places is spatial summation.

41
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.980
For the same local change, inputs near the soma, or on dendrites with a long λ, lose

42
00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:53.980
less.

43
00:02:54.333 --> 00:02:58.773
Time and distance decide how much of each input is still there when the cell

44
00:02:58.773 --> 00:03:02.393
decides. The balance point decides what they add up to.
